WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.600
A potassium channel can pass up to about a hundred million potassium ions a second.

2
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.420
Yet for every thousand potassium ions, fewer than one sodium ion gets through.

3
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.300
Sodium is the smaller ion.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:25.680
Its radius is 0.95 Å, against 1.33 Å for potassium.

5
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.180
So the pore cannot be a simple sieve.

6
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:31.713
Think of it as a trade.

7
00:00:32.653 --> 00:00:38.733
In water, every ion wears a coat of water molecules, and stripping that coat costs

8
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:45.373
energy. An ion will enter a narrow pore only if the pore pays the cost back.

9
00:00:46.553 --> 00:00:53.433
In 1998, Roderick MacKinnon's laboratory showed how, with the structure of KcsA, a

10
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:55.693
potassium channel from a soil bacterium.

11
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.087
Four identical subunits surround one pore.

12
00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:06.967
Near the outer end it narrows to a selectivity filter, 12 Å long, too tight

13
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:08.547
for an ion to keep its water.

14
00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:14.827
The filter is lined with carbonyl oxygen atoms from the protein's backbone, set in

15
00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:19.307
rings that surround a potassium ion much as the oxygens of water did.

16
00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:24.547
Potassium swaps water for protein and loses almost nothing.

17
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:32.007
Sodium is too small to touch all the oxygens of a ring at once, and in the 1998

18
00:01:32.007 --> 00:01:36.087
account the filter is held too stiffly to close in on it.

19
00:01:36.687 --> 00:01:42.407
For sodium, leaving water costs more than the filter repays, so it stays outside.

20
00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:49.307
Two potassium ions sit in the filter in single file, and the repulsion between them

21
00:01:49.307 --> 00:01:51.467
pushes one out as the next arrives.

22
00:01:52.047 --> 00:01:57.667
That is the answer for potassium channels; sodium and calcium channels build their

23
00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:58.407
filters differently.
